WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.070
I think you've just got a glimpse of the future
of education.

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:07.140
The runaway success of Khan Academy, which
launched in 2008,

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:12.570
showed the potential for online learning to
revolutionize K-12 education.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:17.900
It meant that a great classroom lecture could
be experienced by anyone anywhere.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:23.620
And learning software from companies like
dreambox could act as a personal tutor.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:27.280
We've developed the intelligent, adaptive
learning technology

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.900
that celebrates student learning.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:33.980
Twelve years later, classrooms mostly look
like they always have

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:40.300
and many teachers are skeptical that technology
can fundamentally remake classroom instruction.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.600
This whole idea of turning over education
to technology takes away the human element.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:52.380
Computers have been used in classrooms since
the 1980s.

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:57.549
By 2000 there was one PC for every five students
in American classrooms.

00:00:57.549 --> 00:01:00.789
In 2008, the same year that Khan Academy launched,

00:01:00.789 --> 00:01:03.820
the legendary business professor Clayton Christensen,

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.540
who passed away last week at the age of 67,

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:11.840
co-authored a book applying his theory of
disruptive innovation to the classroom.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:17.469
When used disruptively, software could solve
the problem of students learning at different speeds.

00:01:17.469 --> 00:01:20.400
While some are bored, others are struggling
to keep up.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.340
Christensen and his co-authors wanted to flip
the structure of the classroom

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.549
and make is student centric.

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:30.540
They would watch online courses given by the
world's best instructors.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:34.420
Educational apps would gamify learning, help
track progress

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.540
and personalize the content.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:43.220
Teachers could then use classroom time to
help students tackle bigger, more conceptual problems.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.860
Christensen and his co-authors predicted that
by 2018

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:50.960
about 50% of high school classes would be
delivered online.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.420
And they dismissed the objection that government
bureaucracies and teachers unions

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.280
would stand in the way of rapid change.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.600
Their prediction didn't come true.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.400
I would say the biggest lesson that I've had
is not that I'm disappointed

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.830
but just that it takes a long time to do this.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.220
Michael Horn, who was one of Christensen's
co-authors,

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:17.500
is a Harvard MBA who specializes in education
technology.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:22.820
You're trying to overthrow literally a century
of policy and practice

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.680
around our current school system

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:26.100
and all of a sudden we come along and say

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.380
'wait a second, we're not doing it the right
way.'

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.760
Schools have continued to integrate computers
into classrooms

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:35.470
but Horn says they haven't brought fundamental
change.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.530
We think that every student, because they're
in fifth grade for example,

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.140
should be doing the exact same thing at the
exact same time

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:44.010
and we should test on those exact same things.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:49.130
We literally fund schools based on the number
of minutes that students sit in seats

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:50.800
not on how much they learn.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.900
Disrupting Class included a plea to philanthropists
to help fund this disruption.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.420
Foundations answered that call but with limited
impact.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:03.280
Take Summit Learning which is backed by Mark
Zuckerberg and Bill Gates.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.780
Its goal was to be in fifty percent of public
schools by 2025.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:14.020
Over one hundred million dollars later, it's
used in just four tenths of one percent.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.840
Although according to the education news site
Chalkbeat

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:20.980
Summit declined the opportunity to be studied
by a team of Harvard researchers

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:23.620
it can point to anecdotal successes.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:27.760
Such as the experience of one public middle
school in Washington D.C.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.320
So we got wind of this program and said like
'hey let's try it.'

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:33.820
Like let's pilot it.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:36.260
Summit Learning flew teachers to California
for training

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:38.960
and covered the entire cost of llthe program.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.520
We saw just student engagement increase

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.820
and actually like behavioral referrals decrease

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.510
because students were engaged 100%.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:52.500
Denise Edwards tied teachers' performance
evaluations to the new online curriculum.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.460
As a teacher you want that control over your
classroom

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:58.370
like this is your space, this is your baby

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:01.670
and like letting go brings about fear.

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:05.080
But Emily Raskin, a math teacher at another
D.C. high school

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:10.160
says that the flipped classroom model ignores
the realities of student behavior.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.410
I think that we look at the next big thing
that looks great to adults

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:15.860
and assume that it's going to be the same
thing for kids.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:19.920
So if you assign them to watch a video, for
example at home one night,

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.540
that is maybe ten minutes long,

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:26.440
by 30 seconds in, if they're not really interested,
they just kind of zone out

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:31.300
and they come to school the next day really
believing that they have watched the video.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:36.260
In some schools where Summit was implemented
parents fought to overturn it.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:39.620
Parents and students often just don't know
what they don't know about school.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.120
They don't know what it could be, what opportunities
they might be missing.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:59.840
The subjects that we cover in the electronic
apps are science, history, geography,

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:04.360
we do music, Spanish, math, art.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:07.930
According to Clayton Christensen's theory
of disruptive innovation

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:11.720
change happens first in the segments of the
market so insignificant

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:14.500
that nobody is really paying attention to
them

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:17.720
but eventually they grow to overtake the industry.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.900
Disrupting Class gives home schoolers as an
example.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:24.000
Melissa Kigin is a mother of five in rural
Maryland.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.320
And I'm not a teacher, I'm not trained to
be a teacher,

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:32.180
I don't think you need to have that in order
to home school if you have apps like these.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:38.300
We're living in this moment I think where
we can reshape the models at the heart of education

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.920
and leverage these tools and technologies.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:45.120
Are we on the eve of seeing the end of the
traditional classroom?

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:49.940
No reason why it really can't happen in every
classroom in America tomorrow.

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:53.340
Or perhaps Christensen and Horn are guilty
as the saying goes

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:58.000
of mistaking a clear view of the future for
a short distance.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:02.400
The current classroom model may survive for
a long time to come

00:06:02.419 --> 00:06:07.710
at least until software gets better at approximating
student-teacher interactions.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:12.500
And when change does occur, chances are it
will look nothing like we had imagined.

